1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Streets are harder than laboratories. A parked van can hide a pedestrian,

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
and a robot that simply keeps walking can make the wrong decision.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
This is STREETWISE.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
We recreated a Central Avenue encounter beside Kanakadhara in Chennai. First, an

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
official Unitree walking policy follows a constant forward command. The pedestrian emerges,

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
and the robot enters the clearance envelope.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,333
Now replay the same encounter with trained navigation. PPO has learned when

8
00:00:32,333 --> 00:00:37,666
to slow down and wait before proceeding. The walking policy itself stays

9
00:00:37,666 --> 00:00:43,000
frozen. Every robot joint you see comes from recorded MuJoCo physics.

10
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
These are actual training measurements from Weights and Biases. On sixty-four unseen

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,000
pedestrian encounters, completed crossings rise from forty-two to sixty-four. Clearance violations fall

12
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,000
from twenty-two to zero, with no robot falls.

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
We then broadened training on a molab Blackwell GPU. The robot now

14
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
chooses lateral movement as well as speed, facing moving cars and a

15
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,000
marked road-defect zone. On the mixed held-out test, completion rises from twenty-eight

16
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,000
to sixty-four out of sixty-four.

17
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,500
A second experiment asks: what might happen next? The learned world model

18
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:27,000
predicts three action-conditioned futures, compares its prediction with a separate simulator, and

19
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,500
updates from experience. A Weave-traced analyst selects the training focus; validation controls

20
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:36,000
promotion.

21
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Marimo exposes the experiment and its evidence. The official Unitree SDK exchanges

22
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
checked commands and feedback with simulated joints. Code, selected checkpoints and startup

23
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
instructions are included, so judges can inspect and rerun the work.

24
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,333
This is simulation, not a physical robot safety claim. STREETWISE helps teams

25
00:01:51,333 --> 00:01:54,666
discover navigation mistakes before street deployment. Robots that learn the street before

26
00:01:54,666 --> 00:01:58,000
they enter it.
